// `number` — the built-in name for **any numeric scalar**: `int`, `float`, `f32`, `f64` and every
// `i8`..`u64` width.
//
// It is the one built-in that desugars to a UNION rather than to a single lattice type, which is
// what makes it cheap: assignability, narrowing and widening all come from `Type::Union` unchanged,
// so naming the set costs a name and nothing else. The alternative — a thirteenth scalar variant —
// would have had to define its relationship to the other twelve from scratch.
//
// The name existed internally long before it was writable: `Type::is_arith_numeric` reasoned about
// this set, and a native signature could declare it (`SigType::Numeric`, what a kernel's scale
// factor takes). A *user* could only spell it as a twelve-member union by hand.
// expect: stdout "2"
// expect: stdout "2.5"
// expect: stdout "2.0"
// expect: stdout "7"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "false"
// expect: stdout "false"
// expect: exit 0
fn id(x: number): number { return x }

// Every numeric kind is accepted — the widening pair and the strict fixed widths alike.
echo id(2)
echo id(2.5)
echo id(2.0f32)
echo id(7i16)

// It narrows at runtime through the union machinery, in both backends. The widths erase (every
// fixed-width integer is an int at runtime, `f64` a float, `f32` alone reified), so this is a test
// of the three runtime kinds, not of twelve.
a: dyn = 41
b: dyn = 1.5f32
c: dyn = "no"
d: dyn = true
echo a is number
echo b is number
echo c is number
echo d is number
